-- title:
--   Discrete kernel bounds transported from mesh $h$ to the unit lattice
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For $h > 0$ let $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ denote the scaled lattice, and let $\mathrm{DiscreteKernelBounds}(\Gamma, \alpha, \Lambda, R_0, L, \omega)$ denote the discrete kernel-bound assumption: the kernel $\omega$, defined on pairs of points and valued in $[0, \infty]$, satisfies the two-sided bounds with exponent $\alpha$, constant $\Lambda$ and radius $R_0$, relative to the configuration $\Gamma$ and the lattice $L$.
--
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$, let $\alpha, \Lambda, R_0 \in \mathbb{R}$, let $h > 0$, and let $\omega : \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d \to [0,\infty]$ satisfy
--   $$\mathrm{DiscreteKernelBounds}\big(\Gamma,\ \alpha,\ \Lambda,\ R_0 h,\ h\mathbb{Z}^d,\ \omega\big).$$
--   Then the rescaled data satisfy the same bounds on the unit lattice:
--   $$\mathrm{DiscreteKernelBounds}\Big(\ \Gamma(h\,\cdot\,),\ \alpha,\ \Lambda,\ R_0,\ \mathbb{Z}^d,\ (x,y) \mapsto h^{\,d+\alpha}\,\omega(hx, hy)\ \Big),$$
--   where $\Gamma(h\,\cdot\,)$ is the configuration $x \mapsto \Gamma(hx)$ pulled back along the dilation.
--
--   The content is that the dilation $x \mapsto hx$, which carries $\mathbb{Z}^d$ onto $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ and multiplies all distances by $h$, multiplies a jump kernel of order $d + \alpha$ by $h^{-d-\alpha}$; compensating by the factor $h^{d+\alpha}$ therefore leaves the class of admissible kernels invariant, with the *same* constants $\alpha$ and $\Lambda$ and with the radius $R_0$ scaled accordingly. This is exactly what makes a single statement on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ yield its $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ version for every mesh, without reproving anything.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The kernel is valued in $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$ (`ℝ≥0∞`) and the scaling factor enters as `ENNReal.ofReal (h ^ ((d:ℝ) + α))`, a real power with the dimension $d$ coerced to a real exponent. Only $h > 0$ is assumed; no sign hypothesis is placed on $\alpha$, $\Lambda$ or $R_0$, which are whatever the bounds predicate constrains them to be.
